<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z" agent="Trae"><diagram id="pipeline" name="Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="2600" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2200" pageHeight="3200" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" /><mxCell id="header" value="SciAtlas Idea Generation Pipeline&#10;Run dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_question/02-&#10;元素周期表终点/full&#10;Topic: Research question: Will the periodic table ever be&#10;complete?&#10;Context: The periodic table currently consists of 118&#10;elements, the last four of which were announced in 2016.&#10;The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry&#10;(IUPAC), which formally verifies, recognizes, and names&#10;new elements, has a challenging task. Its guidelines for&#10;the criteria for element discovery were established 29&#10;years ago, yet in 2018, IUPAC issued an update&#10;illustrating the challenges associated with resolving&#10;whether the periodic table will ever be complete. IUPAC’s&#10;updated report addressed how previously utilized methods&#10;and technologies for discovering new elements were being&#10;supplanted by innovative approaches. As new techniques are&#10;developed, it is possible more elements will be found—the&#10;limitations of human inventiveness may be our only&#10;obstacle.&#10;Status: failed&#10;Failed step: step9" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="40" width="442" height="438" /></mxCell><mxCell id="params" value="Input Parameters&#10;topic: Research question: Will the periodic table ever be&#10;complete?&#10;Context: The periodic table currently consists of 118&#10;elements, the last four of which were announced in 2016.&#10;The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry&#10;(IUPAC), which formally verifies, recognizes, and names&#10;new elements, has a challenging task. Its guidelines for&#10;the criteria for element discovery were established 29&#10;years ago, yet in 2018, IUPAC issued an update&#10;illustrating the challenges associated with resolving&#10;whether the periodic table will ever be complete. IUPAC’s&#10;updated report addressed how previously utilized methods&#10;and technologies for discovering new elements were being&#10;supplanted by innovative approaches. As new techniques are&#10;developed, it is possible more elements will be found—the&#10;limitations of human inventiveness may be our only&#10;obstacle.&#10;domain: Chemistry&#10;resume_from_run_dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_q&#10;uestion/02-元素周期表终点/full&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;&#10;Effective Pipeline Config&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;anchor_top_k: 5&#10;keyword_min_papers: 2&#10;k_step1: 4&#10;seed_num_queries: 8&#10;seed_per_query: 3&#10;seed_request_multiplier: 6&#10;seed_request_floor: 12&#10;seed_recent_years: 7&#10;seed_min_citations: 1000&#10;graph_budget_ratio: 0.35&#10;graph_budget_min: 12&#10;graph_budget_max: 30&#10;graph_max_predecessors_per_paper: 3&#10;graph_min_forward_papers: 3&#10;graph_candidate_fetch_limit: 40&#10;num_candidate_step2: 3&#10;num_expansion_step2: 1&#10;innoeval_root: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/SciAtlas/referen&#10;ces/search/src/innoeval_search&#10;grobid_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8070&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_same_field: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;max_novelty_feedback_rounds: 2&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;runs_dir: runs&#10;&#10;Effective SciAtlas Config&#10;base_url: http://sciatlas.openkg.cn&#10;api_key: ***REDACTED***&#10;timeout: 900&#10;max_retries: 4&#10;retry_backoff: 5.0&#10;use_cache: True&#10;cache_dir: runs/cache&#10;official_cli_root: .&#10;use_local_kg: False&#10;local_search_root: references/search" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="40" width="442" height="1212" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step1" value="Step 1 Anchor Retrieval&#10;status: resumed&#10;seed_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="340" width="220" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step2" value="Step 2 Research Graph Construction&#10;status: resumed&#10;graph_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="500" width="274" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step1_step_step2" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="step_step2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step2_graph_title" value="Step 2 Annotated Research Graph&#10;paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0&#10;phase_count: 1&#10;directions: anchor=4, backward=0,&#10;forward=0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=13;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="500" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/4&#10;On the discovery of new elements&#10;(IUPAC/IUPAP Report)&#10;year: 2020&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W3048546628&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="148" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_1" value="Seed Paper 2/4&#10;Entrance channel dependent hot&#10;fusion reactions for superheavy&#10;element synthesis&#10;year: 2020&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W3111464399&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="640" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_1" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;Superheavy Nuclei XVII: 1640 ≤ A&#10;&amp;lt; 1650 Systems&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4415525127&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="148" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;Atmospheric-pressure ion transfer&#10;in a gas flow device connected to&#10;the UniCell buffer gas cell for&#10;superheavy elements chemistry:&#10;simulation studies&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4412961862&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="196" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: resumed&#10;serialized_chars: 7358&#10;trend_chars: 7190" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="968" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: Will the periodic table&#10;ever be complete? Context: The periodic table currently&#10;consists of 118 elements, the last four of which were&#10;announced in 2016. The International Union of Pure and Applied&#10;Chemistry (IUPAC), which formally verifies, recognizes, and&#10;names new elements, has a challenging task. Its guidelines for&#10;the criteria for element discovery were established 29 years&#10;ago, yet in 2018, IUPAC issued an update illustrating the&#10;challenges associated with resolving whether the periodic&#10;table will ever be complete. IUPAC’s updated report addressed&#10;how previously utilized methods and technologies for&#10;discovering new elements were being supplanted by innovative&#10;approaches. As new techniques are developed, it is possible&#10;more elements will be found—the limitations of human&#10;inventiveness may be our only obstacle. seed_paper: On the&#10;discovery of new elements (IUPAC/IUPAP Report) # CITATION&#10;SUBGRAPH (3 refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1] Entrance&#10;channel dependent hot fusion reactions for superheavy element&#10;synthesis (2020, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth =&#10;1 cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 58 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unknown is_influential = True low_confidence =&#10;True cited_by_subgraph = 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[]&#10;delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: We have&#10;studied the compound nucleus probability, the survival&#10;probability, and the evaporation residue cross sections for&#10;6645 projectile-target combinations to synthesize the&#10;superheavy nuclei with atomic numbers $Z=110$--126 in the&#10;light of the entrance channel effects such as mass asymmetry,&#10;cha Existing Methods: Unavailable from local metadata.&#10;Motivation: We have studied the compound nucleus probability,&#10;the survival probability, and the evaporation residue cross&#10;sections for 6645 projectile-target combinations to synthesize&#10;the superheavy nuclei with atomic numbers $Z=110$--126 in the&#10;light of the entrance channel effects such as mass asymmetry,&#10;cha Proposed Method: We have studied the compound nucleus&#10;probability, the survival probability, and the evaporation&#10;residue cross sections for 6645 projectile-target combinations&#10;to synthesize the superheavy nuclei with atomic numbers&#10;$Z=110$--126 in the light of the entrance channel effects such&#10;as mass asymmetry, cha Experiment Plan: Unavailable from local&#10;metadata. [ABSTRACT] We have studied the compound nucleus&#10;probability, the survival probability, and the evaporation&#10;residue cross sections for 6645 projectile-target combinations&#10;to synthesize the superheavy nuclei with atomic numbers&#10;$Z=110$--126 in the light of the entrance channel effects such&#10;as mass asymmetry, charge asymmetry, isospin asymmetry, non-&#10;compound nuclear fission probability, and Businaro-Gallone&#10;mass asymmetry. The role of quasifission in every reaction has&#10;also been considered. Decay chains of various superheavy&#10;nuclei produced from the reactions are portrayed as well. The&#10;most striking results are that the measured evaporation&#10;residue cross sections are of the order of picobarns, whereas&#10;the proposed reactions can yield up to microbarn cross&#10;sections for the superheavy nuclei with $110\ensu...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: Paper 0 to Paper 1: Paper 0 is the 2020 study of&#10;entrance-channel-dependent hot fusion reactions for&#10;superheavy-element synthesis. It treats the search for&#10;elements beyond Z = 118 as a reaction-optimization problem: by&#10;surveying thousands of projectile–target combinations, it&#10;evaluates compound-nucleus formation probability, survival&#10;probability, evaporation-residue cross sections, quasifission&#10;effects, and entrance-channel asymmetries for Z = 110–126. Its&#10;central claim is that carefully chosen hot-fusion routes could&#10;produce superheavy nuclei with higher cross sections than many&#10;previously measured cases, potentially opening access to the&#10;eighth row of the periodic table. Paper 1, the 2025&#10;“Superheavy Nuclei XVII: 1640 ≤ A &lt; 1650 Systems” work, moves&#10;the question from “which reactions might synthesize new&#10;nuclei?” to “which nuclear interactions and models can&#10;credibly describe such extreme systems?” It emphasizes that&#10;predictions in the superheavy mass region depend on&#10;extrapolating nuclear interactions from better-known nuclei.&#10;The transition is therefore from practical synthesis&#10;feasibility to theoretical reliability: Paper 0 identifies&#10;candidate pathways to new elements, while Paper 1 asks whether&#10;the underlying nuclear-structure framework is sufficiently&#10;robust to predict stability, decay behavior, and&#10;interpretability for those candidates. Paper 1 to Paper 2:&#10;Paper 2, the 2025 simulation study of atmospheric-pressure ion&#10;transfer in a gas-flow device connected to the UniCell buffer&#10;gas cell, shifts the emphasis from nuclear production and&#10;stability to experimental handling and chemical&#10;identification. It addresses a major bottleneck for elements&#10;beyond Fl/Z = 114: superheavy atoms are produced as rare&#10;energetic recoils, often with very short half-lives and low&#10;yields, and must be thermalized, transported, and chemically&#10;characterized rapidly. The advance is not another fusion&#10;reaction calculation but an infrastructure-level response:&#10;modeling gas-flow and ion-transfer conditions that could&#10;preserve and deliver single atoms for chemical study. This&#10;transition shows the field moving from “can we make and&#10;predict the nucleus?” to “can we capture, transport, and&#10;chemically identify it before it decays?” The two 2025 papers&#10;are best viewed as parallel developments: one strengthens the&#10;theoretical description of superheavy stability, while the&#10;oth..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="968" width="560" height="2094" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: resumed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3162" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Superheavy-element science concerned with the&#10;possible completion of the periodic table, including synthesis&#10;of elements beyond Z = 118, nuclear-stability prediction,&#10;single-atom chemical handling, and discovery verification.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Existing methods do not provide an integrated,&#10;experimentally anchored way to determine whether future&#10;elements can be synthesized, survive long enough, be captured&#10;and chemically identified as single atoms, and satisfy&#10;discovery criteria. Hot-fusion reaction studies can propose&#10;candidate pathways, but quasifission, tiny cross sections, and&#10;survival uncertainties remain. Superheavy-nucleus theory must&#10;extrapolate nuclear interactions into poorly constrained&#10;regions, while chemical-transport simulations show that&#10;identification becomes a time-critical single-atom problem.&#10;The field therefore lacks a unified predictive and verifiable&#10;framework linking reaction choice, nuclear stability, decay&#10;observables, rapid chemical handling, and atomic-number&#10;assignment for elements beyond Z = 118.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- periodic table&#10;- superheavy elements&#10;- elements beyond Z = 118&#10;- atomic number Z&#10;- projectile-target combinations&#10;- hot fusion reactions&#10;- compound nucleus&#10;- evaporation residue cross sections&#10;- survival probability&#10;- quasifission&#10;- entrance-channel asymmetry&#10;- mass asymmetry&#10;- charge asymmetry&#10;- isospin asymmetry&#10;- non-compound nuclear fission probability&#10;- decay chains&#10;- superheavy nuclei&#10;- nuclear interaction models&#10;- nuclear stability&#10;- fission barriers&#10;- island of stability&#10;- half-life&#10;- production rate&#10;- energetic recoil atoms&#10;- gas-filled chamber&#10;- buffer gas cell&#10;- atmospheric-pressure ion transfer&#10;- gas flow device&#10;- thermalization&#10;- single-atom chemical identification&#10;- element-specific chemical behavior&#10;- IUPAC discovery criteria&#10;- atomic-number assignment&#10;- experimental verification&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- projectile-target combinations determine entrance-channel&#10;conditions for hot fusion reactions&#10;- entrance-channel conditions affect compound-nucleus&#10;formation probability&#10;- quasifission competes with compound-nucleus formation and&#10;reduces superheavy-element yield&#10;- compound-nucleus survival probability determines evaporation&#10;residue cross sections&#10;- evaporation residue cross sections determine production&#10;rates of new superheavy nuclei&#10;- nuclear interaction models predict stability, decay&#10;behavior, and fission properties of superheavy nuclei&#10;- predicted stability influences which isotopes are viable&#10;targets for discovery&#10;- decay chains provide indirect evidence of atomic number&#10;- energetic recoil atoms must be thermalized in gas before&#10;chemical study&#10;- gas flow and ion transfer systems transport thermalized&#10;atoms to chemical identification stages&#10;- short half-lives constrain the time available for chemical&#10;separation and detection&#10;- chemical behavior can help assign an element's position in&#10;the periodic table&#10;- IUPAC discovery criteria require sufficient evidence to&#10;recognize a new element&#10;- low production rates and short lifetimes make atomic-number&#10;assignment harder&#10;- theoretical extrapolation affects confidence in predictions&#10;for unknown superheavy systems&#10;- synthesis feasibility, chemical detectability, and discovery&#10;criteria jointly determine whether the periodic table can be&#10;extended&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- extremely low superheavy-element production cross sections&#10;- quasifission suppressing complete compound-nucleus formation&#10;- entrance-channel effects making reaction outcomes difficult&#10;to optimize&#10;- uncertain extrapolation of nuclear interactions into the&#10;superheavy region&#10;- limited experimental anchors for superheavy masses,&#10;lifetimes, and fission properties&#10;- very short half-lives of elements beyond known regions&#10;- low event rates producing only a few atoms&#10;- rapid thermalization and transport of single recoil atoms&#10;before decay&#10;- difficulty of performing chemical identification on single&#10;atoms&#10;- ambiguous interpretation of decay-chain evidence when yields&#10;are low&#10;- lack of fully integrated synthesis-to-verification pathways&#10;- unclear scientific boundary for periodic-table completion&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- optimizing reactions for higher yield versus reaching&#10;longer-lived isotopes near the island of stability&#10;- using theoretical extrapolation to guide experiments versus&#10;needing experimental data to constrain theory&#10;- synthesizing heavier nuclei versus retaining enough lifetime&#10;for chemical verification&#10;- requiring strict discovery evidence versus having only a few&#10;detectable atoms&#10;- relying on nuclear decay signatures versus needing chemical&#10;or spectroscopic element-specific proof&#10;- treating element discovery as a synthesis problem versus&#10;treating it as an integrated detection and verification&#10;problem&#10;- defining completion by nuclear instability versus defining&#10;it by experimental detectability or discovery standards&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- predicts feasible synthesis pathways for elements beyond Z =&#10;118&#10;- accounts for entrance-channel effects, quasifission,&#10;compound-nucleus formation, and survival probability&#10;- quantifies uncertainty in cross sections, lifetimes, decay&#10;modes, and stability predictions&#10;- connects nuclear-structure predictions to measurable&#10;observables&#10;- identifies reactions or isotopes that may permit longer-&#10;lived nuclei suitable for verification&#10;- supports rapid thermalization, transport, and single-atom&#10;chemical handling&#10;- enables element-specific identification through decay,&#10;chemistry, X-rays, or spectroscopy&#10;- provides robust criteria for assigning atomic number under&#10;low-count conditions&#10;- distinguishes physical limits of nuclear stability from&#10;experimental and verification limits&#10;- can be tested against existing or near-future superheavy-&#10;element facilities&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- coupled reaction-dynamics and nuclear-structure prediction&#10;frameworks&#10;- uncertainty-quantified extrapolation of nuclear interactions&#10;- multi-observable constraint mechanisms using decay chains,&#10;masses, fission properties, and chemical signatures&#10;- rapid recoil thermalization and gas-phase ion transport&#10;mechanisms&#10;- single-atom capture and time-resolved chemical interrogation&#10;mechanisms&#10;- element-specific identification mechanisms based on decay&#10;correlation, X-ray emission, or atomic spectroscopy&#10;- statistical or decision-theoretic discovery-criterion&#10;mechanisms for few-atom evidence&#10;- experiment-design mechanisms that prioritize reactions&#10;likely to yield verifiable, longer-lived nuclei&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- heavy-ion fusion and superheavy-element synthesis facilities&#10;- rare-isotope beam and recoil-separator experiments&#10;- single-atom chemistry and atom-at-a-time detection&#10;- gas-jet transport, buffer-gas stopping, and aerosol&#10;transport systems&#10;- microfluidic or capillary rapid chemical separation&#10;- low-count radiation detection and decay-chain correlation&#10;methods&#10;- X-ray and atomic spectroscopy of heavy ions&#10;- Bayesian calibration and uncertainty quantification for&#10;nuclear models&#10;- active learning or Bayesian experimental design for reaction&#10;selection&#10;- relativistic quantum chemistry for periodic trends in&#10;superheavy elements&#10;- rare-event detection, nuclear forensics, and trace-analysis&#10;methods" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3162" width="560" height="3588" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: resumed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6850" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed because the core&#10;problem remains anchored in superheavy-element synthesis,&#10;nuclear stability, recoil handling, and discovery criteria.&#10;Cross-domain retrieval is also justified because key&#10;bottlenecks—few-atom evidence, rapid single-atom transport,&#10;uncertainty-quantified extrapolation, and robust atomic-number&#10;assignment—may benefit from mechanism transfer from rare-event&#10;detection, trace analysis, Bayesian experimental design, or&#10;rapid micro-scale chemical handling." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6850" width="560" height="348" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: resumed&#10;candidate_count: 6" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="7298" width="239" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Colliding heavy nuclei take multiple identities on the&#10;path to fusion&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: The properties of superheavy elements probe extremes&#10;of physics and chemistry. They are synthesised at accelerator&#10;laboratories using nuclear fusion, where two atomic nuclei&#10;collide, stick together (capture), then with low probability&#10;evolve to a compact superheavy nucleus. Th...&#10;&#10;can